1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,380
A robot learns a skill.

2
00:00:02,720 --> 00:00:05,260
But that skill stays locked inside its body.

3
00:00:05,740 --> 00:00:07,420
The next robot starts from zero.

4
00:00:08,360 --> 00:00:09,980
And you can't just copy it over.

5
00:00:10,280 --> 00:00:11,700
A Panda has seven joints.

6
00:00:12,060 --> 00:00:13,900
A UR5e has six.

7
00:00:14,340 --> 00:00:16,480
Same task, totally different motion.

8
00:00:17,540 --> 00:00:19,700
Nimbotics is a shared memory for robots.

9
00:00:20,200 --> 00:00:22,200
One robot learns a task in simulation.

10
00:00:22,560 --> 00:00:25,940
It saves the task and the route that worked, but not its joint angles.

11
00:00:26,340 --> 00:00:28,920
A different robot then works out its own motion.

12
00:00:29,380 --> 00:00:31,540
And physics decides what gets saved.

13
00:00:34,350 --> 00:00:35,210
Here's the Lab.

14
00:00:35,330 --> 00:00:39,230
I pick the UR5e and describe the job in plain words.

15
00:00:41,140 --> 00:00:46,660
Meta's Muse Spark reads my request and the whole library in one call, and finds the matching task.

16
00:00:47,020 --> 00:00:48,580
Reach between two posts.

17
00:00:49,580 --> 00:00:53,360
If it isn't sure, it shows me the closest match and asks a question.

18
00:00:53,920 --> 00:00:56,500
The UR5e has never done this task.

19
00:00:56,720 --> 00:00:59,680
But the Capsule arm, a robot someone uploaded, has.

20
00:01:00,200 --> 00:01:04,440
So the UR5e learns from it, and Muse decides which routes to try first.

21
00:01:05,980 --> 00:01:10,490
Muse can look at both robots and why each route failed, but it can only read.

22
00:01:10,540 --> 00:01:11,960
It never moves the robot.

23
00:01:12,320 --> 00:01:17,780
The UR5e solves its own motion, and the MuJoCo simulator tests the top three routes.

24
00:01:18,680 --> 00:01:21,580
Less than a millimetre off target, and it touched nothing.

25
00:01:21,780 --> 00:01:25,300
Only then is the skill saved, linked back to the Capsule arm.

26
00:01:26,180 --> 00:01:27,680
Every Muse call is logged.

27
00:01:27,840 --> 00:01:30,960
This run used two calls, for less than a tenth of a cent.

28
00:01:31,280 --> 00:01:35,700
And this time, Muse picked the same route our basic rule would have.

29
00:01:36,200 --> 00:01:39,720
Muse can also design a brand new task from one sentence.

30
00:01:40,020 --> 00:01:42,900
Our backend checks the layout before anything runs.

31
00:01:43,500 --> 00:01:45,230
But Muse can't make a task possible.

32
00:01:45,280 --> 00:01:49,380
Here, no route passed the physics test, so nothing was saved.

33
00:01:50,460 --> 00:01:51,150
So Muse thinks.

34
00:01:51,200 --> 00:01:56,240
It matches goals, designs tasks, ranks routes, and sums up the library.

35
00:01:56,980 --> 00:01:57,680
Physics proves.

36
00:01:58,020 --> 00:02:01,020
Nothing becomes a skill until the simulator checks it.

37
00:02:01,640 --> 00:02:04,160
We tested this on twenty new scenes.

38
00:02:04,760 --> 00:02:09,430
Sharing matched planning from scratch, seventeen out of twenty, while plain retargeting solved ten.

39
00:02:09,480 --> 00:02:13,680
It cut the search on eleven scenes, and made it worse on six.

40
00:02:14,400 --> 00:02:17,000
It's live at nimbotics dot net.

41
00:02:17,100 --> 00:02:23,680
Anyone can upload their own robot, even humanoids, two armed and mobile robots, and see which robot taught which.

42
00:02:24,120 --> 00:02:25,330
One robot learns it.

43
00:02:25,380 --> 00:02:26,480
Every robot remembers.

44
00:02:26,700 --> 00:02:27,820
This is Nimbotics.
